74 16 66 90 66 90 C6 C3 79 F6 DD 80 C1 F3 F6 D8 | t.f.f...y.......
0F 85 AD 8B 01 00 56 E8 74 17 01 00 7B ED 50 81 | ......V.t...{.P.
C0 90 75 2C 82 E8 53 28 00 00 80 E8 BD C7 C1 42 | ..u,..S(.......B
DWORD[0] = 0x02F8248C (global decryption key, session 2)
DWORD[0] = 0xC9EF16A9 (global decryption key, session 1)

The key changes every game session, This prevents:
Offline analysis of encrypted HWID strings
Static decryption of the .ei` section
Replay attacks with captured HWID data

8.5 XOR Based String Decryption ( Reverse engineered Algortihm )

Code:
std::vector<uint8_t> decrypt_eidolon_string(const std::vector<uint8_t>& encrypted_bytes, uint32_t global_key) {
    uint32_t k1 = (global_key ^ 0x4A772405) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
    uint32_t k2 = (k1 + 0x37022887) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
    uint32_t k3 = (k2 ^ 0x127F17B3) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
    uint32_t k4 = (k3 - global_key) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
 
    std::vector<uint8_t> result;
    result.reserve(encrypted_bytes.size());
    uint32_t rolling_key = k1;
 
    for (size_t i = 0; i < encrypted_bytes.size(); i++) {
        uint8_t k_byte = (rolling_key >> ((i % 4) * 8)) & 0xFF;
        uint8_t k_byte2 = (k3 >> ((i % 4) * 8)) & 0xFF;
        uint8_t decrypted_byte = encrypted_bytes[i] ^ k_byte ^ k_byte2 ^ (i & 0xFF);
        result.push_back(decrypted_byte);
 
        rolling_key = ((rolling_key << 3) | (rolling_key >> 29)) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
        rolling_key ^= k2;
    }
 
    return result;
}

9, HWID Collection Chain

9.1 Complete HWID Sources

Eidolon collects hardware identifiers from 6 distinct sources:

CPU Information:

Path: HKLM\HARDWARE\DESCRIPTION\System\CentralProcessor\0
Value: ProcessorNameString
API: ADVAPI32!RegOpenKeyExW + RegQueryValueExW
Data: "Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-14900K" (example)

Registry, Machine GUID:

Path: HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Cryptography
Value: MachineGuid
API: ADVAPI32!RegOpenKeyExW + RegQueryValueExW
Data: "d4ebc8a5-3c4f-4a7b-9e12-8f1a2b3c4d5e" (example)

Disk Hardware ID:

Path: HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Class\{4d36e967-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\0
Value: HardwareID
API: ADVAPI32!RegOpenKeyExW + RegQueryValueExW
Data: "SCSI\Disk_______WD_BLACK_SN850X____" (example)

MAC Address:

API: IPHLPAPI!GetAdaptersInfo
Data: MAC address (6 bytes) and adapter description


Peripheral Identifiers:

API: HID!HidD_GetProductString
Data: Product strings from HID devices (keyboards, mice, etc.)


Volume Serial:

API: KERNEL32!GetVolumeInformationW
Data: Volume serial number, filesystem name


Computer Name:

API: KERNEL32!GetComputerNameW
Data: NetBIOS computer name


User Name:

API: KERNEL32!GetUserNameW
Data: Current user account name


9.2 HWID Collection Flow
1- String decryption, RVA 0xEEA40 decrypts registry path strings
2- Registry Enumeration, RegOpenKeyExW -> RegQueryValueExW -> RegCloseKey
3- Additional HWID Sources, GetAdaptersInfo, HidD_GetProductString, GetVolumeInformationW, GetComputerNameW, GetUserNameW.
4- String Concatenation, All values joined into canonical string
5- SHA 1 Hashing, First hash pass (constants @ RVA 0x1BD7F1A)
6- RC4 Encryption, Custom RC4 with modified KSA (key @ RVA 0x2599931)
7- RSA Encryption, Blizzard public key → encrypted HWID blob
8- Base64 Encoding, CryptStringToBinaryW -> final payload
9- Sent to Auth Server, Via HTTPS in game connection flow


9.4 HWID Encryption Keys

The SHA-1 and RC4 implementations use hardcoded constants found at these locations in Overwatch.exe:

SHA-1 Constants (@ RVA 0x1BD7F1A):

SHA-1 K[0] = 0x5A827999
SHA-1 K[1] = 0x6ED9EBA1
SHA-1 K[2] = 0x8F1BBCDC
SHA-1 K[3] = 0xCA62C1D6

RC4 Implementation (@ RVA 0x2599931)
Custom Key Scheduling Algorithm (KSA) with additional permutation rounds
Standard 256-byte S-box initialization
Modified to include a round-dependent counter value in the swap operation
Makes standard RC4 attack techniques less effective


10, PEB Based Checks


Warden checks these PEB fields (detected via TLS callbacks):

PEB Structure (x64):
+0x002: BYTE BeingDebugged -> 0 = not debugged, 1 = debugged
+0x003: BYTE BitField -> Contains ImageUsedOs2 flag
+0x0BC: ULONG NtGlobalFlag -> 0 = normal, non-zero = debugger flags


Detection methods that Warden can use:
1. IsDebuggerPresent() reads PEB.BeingDebugged
2. CheckRemoteDebuggerPresent() queries csrss.exe
3. NtQueryInformationProcess(ProcessDebugPort) checks debug port
4. NtQueryInformationProcess(ProcessDebugFlags) checks debug flags
5. NtQueryInformationProcess(ProcessDebugObjectHandle) checks debug object


11.3 Timing Detection

Warden uses GetTickCount64 to detect debugger-induced slowdowns:

Method**: Take timestamp → execute sensitive code -> take timestamp -> compare delta
Expected delta: Normal (sub-millisecond for simple operations)
Detection threshold: Delta > expected × 2 (heuristic) -> debugger slowdown


11.4 Control Flow Flattening (Eidolon)

Eidolon uses control-flow flattening to obfuscate function logic:

Normal flow:
if (cond) { A } else { B }; C

Flattened flow:
state = 0
while (1) {
switch (state) {
case 0: state = cond ? 1 : 2; break;
case 1: A; state = 3; break;
case 2: B; state = 3; break;
case 3: C; return;
}
}


11.5 Global Decryption Key (Rotating)

Session 1: 0xC9EF16A9 (first runtime scan)
Session 2: 0x02F8248C (second runtime scan, diffrent session )


11.6 Process Debug Port

NtQueryInformationProcess(ProcessDebugPort):
DebugPort = 0 (no debugger attached)

